# Added 2026-11-23, defect 2679, lane-p255-impl (panel 255's R1 and R5): the
# runtime says which route decides a jump out of a signal handler, by
# `hero_signal_jump_route()`: bit 1 when a jump of the `setjmp` family reads
# the stack pointer it will land at from the one word of its buffer (route C,
# the key verified before `main`), bit 2 when a context jump reads it from
# the context's own field, 3 when both do, and 0 where each falls to the
# record that reads only memory which provably lives (route A).
# Why 3 here: the calibration verifies the C library's layout on each
# platform the CI runs a POSIX leg on (panel 255's R1, measured on glibc 2.41
# and 2.43 and on macOS 27, arm64 and x86-64 for glibc). A platform whose
# layout does not verify answers lower and says so by this number (glibc's
# other architectures, Darwin x86-64). Windows has no route to ask. The
# forcing define, `HERO_JMP_FORCE_UNCALIBRATED` (1 the key, 2 the contexts, 3
# both), is run apart, in the lane that landed this: the commit says which.
extern "fixedbugs-2679-the-runtime-says-which-route-decides-a-jump.h"
    function route_now() -> i64

function main()
    print("route ", route_now())
